data: {"id":"chatcmpl-c0901750-bac9-4506-96f3-4680063e7da0","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790745084,"model":"capitoline-judge-sol61","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"**Favor EC-X for long-lived, rarely modified backups; use plain RF2 when frequent changes or performance requirements outweigh capacity savings.** Six nodes and RF2 alone do not settle the choice.\n\nEC-X is enabled on an RF2 container; it does not replace the replication policy. New data initially remains replicated, and eligible cold data is encoded later. The decision therefore depends on retention, overwrite frequency, backup and restore windows, available CPU/I/O headroom, and guidance for your AOS version and backup software.\n\n- **Plain RF2 costs capacity:** approximately 2 TB raw per 1 TB logical, before compression or deduplication. It avoids background encoding and reconstruction overhead, generally providing more predictable performance for changing data and recovery.\n- **EC-X trades processing work for capacity:** a typical 4-data-plus-1-parity stripe consumes approximately 1.25 TB raw per 1 TB logical—37.5% less than RF2 for fully encoded data. Actual savings depend on eligibility and stripe formation. Encoding consumes CPU, I/O, and network resources; degraded reads and rebuilds require reconstruction. Repeated backup merges and overwrites can reduce savings and increase work.\n\nBoth retain the RF2 protection target of one failure within the configured fault-domain policy; EC-X does not confer RF3 protection. Keep rebuild capacity reserves either way.\n\nFor stable backup retention, EC-X is usually attractive. Validate the choice with representative ingest, merge, and restore tests."},"finish_reason":null}]}

